# Disk Temperature Monitor Configuration
# Format: device_path,friendly_name,grep_pattern
#
# The monitor takes field 10 (RAW_VALUE) of the last smartctl -a line
# matching grep_pattern, so the pattern must match an ATA attribute row:
#   - Temperature_Celsius      (attribute 194, most HDDs and SSDs)
#   - Airflow_Temperature_Cel  (attribute 190, some SSDs report only this)
# NVMe and SAS disks print temperature in another layout and read as N/A.
#
# Find your pattern with: sudo smartctl -a /dev/sdX | grep -i temp

# Example configurations (uncomment and edit for your system):

# Two disks example:
# /dev/sdf,DataDisk,Temperature_Celsius
# /dev/sdh,MediaDisk,Temperature_Celsius

# SSD + HDD example:
# /dev/sda,System_SSD,Temperature_Celsius
# /dev/sdb,Backup_HDD,Temperature_Celsius

# RAID array example:
# /dev/sda,RAID_Disk1,Temperature_Celsius
# /dev/sdb,RAID_Disk2,Temperature_Celsius
# /dev/sdc,RAID_Disk3,Temperature_Celsius
# /dev/sdd,RAID_Disk4,Temperature_Celsius
